rtices at $(1,0),(-1,0),(2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(2,1)$" 13194139535264 true] [17592186340531 1113 #uuid "2d1086ef-7377-52e7-a4e5-3b85aa2a5ee6" 13194139535264 true] [17592186340531 1145 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(-1,0),(-2,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340533 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340534 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340535 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340536 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340537 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1041 "selection" 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340536 13194139535264 true] [17592186340532 1110 #uuid "2876b4c0-301c-5020-b0d1-08031e9314c5" 13194139535264 true] [17592186340533 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535264 true] [1759218634